# wget — real-world examples

## Single downloads

```sh
# Download a single file
wget http://path.to.the/file

# Download and rename
wget http://path.to.the/file -O newname

# Download into a directory
wget -P path/to/directory http://path.to.the/file

# Continue an aborted download (huge ISOs, flaky wifi)
wget -c https://download.opensuse.org/tumbleweed/iso/openSUSE-Tumbleweed-DVD-x86_64-Current.iso
```

## Multiple downloads

```sh
# Multiple URLs at once
wget URL1 URL2

# Fetch every URL listed in a file
wget -i url_list.txt

# Download files matching a numeric pattern
wget http://www.myserver.com/files-{1..15}.tar.bz2

# Recursive, one level deep, only a given extension (needs directory indexing)
wget -r -l1 -A.pdf http://myserver.com/directory
```

## Mirroring

```sh
# Mirror one page locally with everything needed to view it
wget -pk http://path.to.the/page.html

# Mirror a whole site locally
wget -mk http://site.tl/
```

## Inspection and identity

```sh
# Fetch only the response headers, print to stdout
wget -S --spider -O - http://google.com

# Change the User-Agent
wget -U 'toto' http://google.com
```

## Homelab uses

```sh
# Quietly pull a nightly config backup to homelab (cron/systemd-timer friendly)
wget -q -O /srv/backups/router-$(date +%F).cfg http://192.168.1.1/backup.cfg

# Check whether Plex on mediaserver is answering, headers only
wget -S --spider http://mediaserver:32400/web/ 2>&1 | head -15

# Rate-limit a big grab so it doesn't choke movie night
wget --limit-rate=2m -c https://example.com/big-dataset.tar.gz
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-O FILE` | save as FILE (`-O -` = stdout) |
| `-P DIR` | save into DIR |
| `-c` | continue partial download |
| `-i FILE` | read URLs from FILE |
| `-q` | quiet |
| `-r -l N` | recursive, N levels deep |
| `-A EXT` | accept only matching files |
| `-m` | mirror (recursive + timestamps) |
| `-p` | fetch page requisites (css, images) |
| `-k` | convert links for local viewing |
| `-S --spider` | headers only, download nothing |
| `-U AGENT` | set User-Agent |
| `--limit-rate=2m` | throttle bandwidth |
